    a: "<b>Medication Reconciliation at Transition of Care:</b><ol><li><b>Obtain complete medication list:</b><ul><li>All prescription medications (including those prescribed by specialists)</li><li>OTC medications, vitamins, supplements, herbal products</li><li>Medications previously held or stopped in hospital</li></ul></li><li><b>Compare hospital medication list with pre-admission list</b> — identify:<ul><li>New medications added (with indication explained)</li><li>Medications discontinued (with reason)</li><li>Dose changes and their rationale</li><li>Medications held (e.g., metformin held for contrast — when to restart)</li></ul></li><li><b>Address high-risk medications</b> specifically (anticoagulants, insulin, diuretics, antihypertensives — especially if doses changed)</li><li><b>Teach-back</b> — patient/caregiver demonstrates understanding of each medication's purpose and administration</li><li><b>Provide written medication list</b> in patient's primary language at appropriate literacy level</li><li><b>Ensure timely prescription fulfillment</b> — Rx sent to pharmacy, medications available before leaving hospital</li><li><b>Communicate reconciled list to next provider</b> (PCP, SNF, home health) — discharge summary must include</li></ol><b>Pearl:</b> Medication errors at discharge cause 20% of adverse events in the post-acute period"
