    q: "How does COVID-19 worsen social isolation among older adults and what lessons were learned for future pandemic preparedness?",
    a: "<b>COVID-19 and Social Isolation in Older Adults:</b><br><b>Mechanisms of Worsening Isolation:</b><ul><li><b>Visitor restrictions in SNFs and ALFs</b> — families prohibited from visiting for months; residents locked in rooms to prevent spread</li><li><b>Community lockdowns</b> — closure of senior centers, adult day programs, congregate meal sites</li><li><b>Cancellation of social programming</b> (church services, volunteer programs, recreational activities)</li><li><b>Fear of infection</b> — self-imposed isolation even after restrictions lifted</li><li><b>Technology barriers</b> — many older adults lacked devices or skills for video communication</li></ul><b>Health Consequences:</b><ul><li>Accelerated cognitive decline (reduced stimulation)</li><li>Depression and anxiety surged in nursing home residents</li><li>Deconditioning from physical inactivity</li><li>Malnutrition (disrupted food delivery systems)</li><li>Excess deaths (not only from COVID) attributable to isolation effects</li></ul><b>Lessons for Future Preparedness:</b><ul><li>Designate essential visitor/caregiver access even during lockdowns</li><li>Technology infrastructure (tablets, WiFi in SNFs) must be funded in advance</li><li>Telehealth training for elderly before crises occur</li><li>Outdoor visiting structures and safe visitation protocols</li><li>Mental health monitoring protocols for SNF residents during public health emergencies</li></ul>"
