    q: "What is the 'Age-Friendly Health System' initiative and the 4Ms framework? How does it apply to geriatric care in all settings?",
    a: "<b>Age-Friendly Health System (AFHS) Initiative:</b><ul><li>Launched by the <b>Institute for Healthcare Improvement (IHI)</b> and the John A. Hartford Foundation</li><li>Evidence-based framework to make all health system interactions safe and effective for older adults</li><li>Over 3,000 health systems participating; both hospital and ambulatory settings</li></ul><b>The 4Ms Framework:</b><ol><li><b>What Matters:</b><ul><li>Know and align care with each older adult's specific health outcome goals and care preferences</li><li>Advance care planning; shared decision-making; goals of care</li></ul></li><li><b>Medication:</b><ul><li>If medication is necessary, use Age-Friendly medications that do not interfere with What Matters, Mentation, or Mobility</li><li>Deprescribing; Beers/STOPP criteria; avoid anticholinergics, benzos, NSAIDs</li></ul></li><li><b>Mentation:</b><ul><li>Prevent, identify, treat, and manage dementia, depression, and delirium across care settings</li><li>Cognitive screening; delirium prevention protocols</li></ul></li><li><b>Mobility:</b><ul><li>Ensure safe mobility to maintain function and do What Matters</li><li>Early mobilization in hospital; fall prevention; physical therapy</li></ul></li></ol><b>SDOH connection:</b> 'What Matters' explicitly incorporates social needs and patient values into care planning"
