    q: "What are the specific provisions of OBRA 1987 regarding chemical restraints in nursing homes?",
    a: "<b>OBRA 1987 — Chemical Restraints in Nursing Homes:</b><ul><li>A chemical restraint is defined as a medication used to <b>control behavior or restrict the resident's freedom of movement and is not required to treat the resident's medical symptoms</b></li></ul><b>Key Regulatory Requirements:</b><ol><li>Medications <b>cannot be used for convenience of staff</b> or as punishment</li><li>Antipsychotics require a <b>specific psychiatric diagnosis</b> justifying use</li><li>Gradual dose reduction (GDR) must be attempted unless clinically contraindicated</li><li>Facility must document why GDR was not attempted if skipped</li><li>Behavior monitoring required when antipsychotics prescribed</li></ol><b>CMS Partnership to Improve Dementia Care in Nursing Homes:</b><ul><li>National campaign to reduce <b>unnecessary antipsychotic use</b> in nursing home residents</li><li>Launched 2012; achieved significant reductions (from ~24% to &lt;14% of residents nationally)</li><li>Included in CMS quality measures (star rating)</li></ul><b>Appropriate uses for antipsychotics in nursing home residents:</b><ul><li>Schizophrenia</li><li>Huntington's disease</li><li>Tourette's disorder</li><li>Brief acute psychosis</li><li>Dementia BPSD with safety risk — after non-pharmacological approaches failed</li></ul><b>Note:</b> Black box warning for antipsychotics in dementia patients (increased mortality)"
