    q: "Describe how income and wealth inequality in later life affect nursing home placement and quality of care received.",
    a: "<b>Income/Wealth Inequality and Nursing Home Care:</b><br><b>Nursing Home Placement:</b><ul><li>Low-income elderly more likely to enter nursing homes at younger ages and for lower levels of dependency</li><li>Reason: less ability to purchase home care alternatives; smaller social support networks</li><li>Higher-income elderly can pay for extensive home care, reducing nursing home need</li></ul><b>Quality Disparities:</b><ul><li><b>Dual-eligible (Medicaid) patients</b> receive care in facilities with lower star ratings on average</li><li>Medicaid-dependent SNFs have:<ul><li>Lower staffing ratios</li><li>Higher rates of deficiency citations</li><li>Less access to private rooms</li><li>Less diverse programming</li></ul></li><li>Private-pay residents may receive preferential room assignments, amenities</li><li>Medicaid reimbursement rates insufficient in many states → facilities serving Medicaid residents have smaller margins</li></ul><b>Racial Disparities:</b><ul><li>Black and Hispanic elderly more likely to be in lower-quality nursing homes (concentration in high-Medicaid facilities)</li><li>Geographic segregation → racial segregation of nursing homes</li><li>Documented disparities in pain management, pressure ulcer care, and restraint use</li></ul><b>Policy response:</b> CMS efforts to equalize quality through enhanced oversight of low-performing facilities"
