    q: "What is dementia caregiver 'anticipatory grief' and how should clinicians address it?",
    a: "<b>Anticipatory Grief in Dementia Caregivers:</b><ul><li>Grief that occurs <b>before</b> the death of a loved one — in response to ongoing progressive losses of the person to dementia</li><li>Also called 'ambiguous loss' (Pauline Boss) — the person is physically present but psychologically absent</li></ul><b>Manifestations:</b><ul><li>Grieving the loss of the person's personality, cognitive abilities, shared memories, and relationship roles</li><li>'I lost my husband years before he died' — common caregiver statement</li><li>Grief for the life and future that was expected</li><li>Guilt about negative feelings (resentment, wishing it were over)</li></ul><b>Complications:</b><ul><li>Anticipatory grief predicts more complex bereavement after death</li><li>Associated with depression, anxiety, and caregiver burden</li></ul><b>Clinician Response:</b><ol><li><b>Normalize the experience</b> — validate that grieving losses before death is normal and expected</li><li><b>Name it</b> — 'What you're feeling is called anticipatory grief; it's very common'</li><li><b>Support group referral</b> — Alzheimer's Association support groups; peer connection with others experiencing same</li><li><b>Individual therapy</b> — grief-focused CBT if depression/anxiety present</li><li><b>Meaning-making</b> — finding purpose and meaning in the caregiving role</li><li><b>Respite care</b> — reduce caregiver exhaustion that compounds grief</li></ol>"
