    q: "What is sundowning in dementia and how does the nursing home environment contribute to it? What management strategies address it?",
    a: "<b>Sundowning (Late-Day Confusion):</b><ul><li>Cluster of behavioral and neuropsychiatric symptoms — agitation, confusion, wandering, aggression — occurring <b>in late afternoon and evening</b> in persons with dementia</li><li>Prevalence: affects ~20% of patients with Alzheimer's disease</li></ul><b>Nursing Home Environmental Contributors:</b><ul><li>Shift changes — multiple unfamiliar faces in late afternoon creates confusion</li><li>Reduced structured activity in late afternoon/evening</li><li>Fatigue accumulated during day</li><li>Poor lighting → altered circadian cues</li><li>Increased noise during dinner preparation and shift change</li><li>Staff familiar to resident going home for the day</li></ul><b>Non-Pharmacological Management (First-Line):</b><ol><li><b>Bright light therapy</b> — morning light exposure (10,000 lux for 1–2 hours) to reinforce circadian rhythm</li><li><b>Structured late-afternoon activities</b> — exercise, music, reminiscence before agitation peak</li><li><b>Environmental modification</b> — reduce stimulation, improve lighting, consistent staff in evening</li><li><b>Consistent daily routine</b> — predictable schedule reduces confusion</li><li><b>Melatonin</b> — 0.5–3 mg at bedtime; evidence limited but reasonable first pharmacologic option</li></ol><b>Pharmacological (only if non-pharm fails and safety at risk):</b><ul><li>Low-dose antipsychotic — with documented indication, GDR plan, black-box warning discussion with family</li></ul>"
